  "strategy": "The problem requires finding a better upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant C. The objective is to minimize the overlap integral C_5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx for h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1.\n\nApproach and rationale:\nWe employ a multi-stage iterative optimization framework with several enhancements. The core idea is:\n1. Use multiple analytical templates as initial conditions\n2. Combine deterministic optimization methods:\n   - Constrained SLSQP with smoothed surrogate\n   - Projected gradient descent\n   - Coordinate descent\n3. Add stochastic elements for escape from local optima\n4. Use geometric interpolation to transfer solutions between resolutions\n5. Incorporate automatic differentiation for gradient computation\n\nThe key improvements over the original code are:\n1. Automatic differentiation for gradient calculation\n2. Structured resolution transfer mechanism\n3. Adaptive hyperparameter scheduling\n4. Enhanced stagnation detection\n5. Better handling of boundary conditions\n\nMathematicalspecification:\n1. Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, \u2211h_i / n = 1/2 (since dx=2/n, reverse engineering to match the integral constraint)\n2. Objective: min max_k (dx * \u2211_{i} h_i * (1-h_{i+k mod N}) ) (Note: mode=\"full\" already returns positive/negative lags, but we need to handle wrapped indices)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: seed (current_h_values for n=360 if available)\n2. Resolution scanning:\n   - Start with parent resolution, then 480, 720 points (time-dependent)\n3. For each resolution:\n   a. Validation: append parent transferred to current resolution\n   b. Generation: \n      i. Uniform tiles (geometric)\n      ii. Wave templates (sinusoidal)\n      iii. Harmonic content variations\n   c. Each generation uses interpolation artifacts\n4. Optimization pipeline:\n   i. SLSQP with adapted Euclidean form\n       - Smoothed surrogate: max_k * (1 + \u03b1*exp((value-clip)/\u03b1))^{-\u03b3}\n       - \u03b3=3 for intermediate smoothing\n       - Jacobian provided via forward-mode AD\n   ii. Projected gradient with adaptive step size:\n        - Uses backtracking linesearch\n        - Strict feasibility with projection at every step\n   iii. Coordinate descent with adaptive sweeping (order by lag contribution)\n5. Budget allocation:\n   - 40% to exploration (multiple initial conditions)\n   - 30% to optimization (complexity-aware)\n   - 30% to finalization (validation)\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Base templates (geometric/analytical)\n2. Stochastic variations (frequency/random)\n3. Resolution hierarchies (360\u2192480\u2192720)\n4. Template composition (mixed-mode)\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n1. Resolution changes: linear interpolation with L2 projection\n2. Timers set with exponential decay in budget allocation\n3. Graceful degradation for gradient abnormalities\n4. Fall back to previous best at return\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Check sum = 0.5 * n (within 1e-12)\n2. Re-evaluate objective with original correlation\n3. Output: (h_values, c5_value, n_points)\n\nResolution transfer mechanism:\n1. Linear interpolation: for transferred h_prev to n_new,\n   h_new[i] = (1 - i/(n_new-1)) * h_prev[i_old] + (i/(n_new-1)) * h_prev[i_old+1]\nwhere i_old = floor(n * i/(n_new-1)) needed to adjust indices consistently.\n\nFull implementation with helper functions and logging included. The code structure follows the rigorous optimization trajectory with adaptive options handling and detailed reporting.",
